What are Process States?
A process state represents the current status of a process during its lifecycle. Since the CPU can execute only a limited number of processes at a time, the operating system moves processes between different states.

        +------+
        | New  |
        +------+
            |
            v
        +-------+
        | Ready |
        +-------+
            |
            v
        +---------+
        | Running |
        +---------+
         /       \
        /         \
       v           v
 +---------+   +------------+
 | Waiting |   | Terminated |
 +---------+   +------------+
       |
       v
    Ready
    
1. New State
	The process has just been created.
2. Ready State
	The process is ready to run but is waiting for CPU allocation.
3. Running State
	The CPU is currently executing the process instructions.
4. Waiting (Blocked) State
	The process cannot continue until some event occurs.
5. Terminated State
	The process has finished execution.
	
A process state indicates the current status of a process in its lifecycle. The main states are New, Ready, Running, Waiting (Blocked), and Terminated. A process starts in the New state, moves to Ready when it's prepared to execute, enters Running when assigned the CPU, goes to Waiting if it needs an I/O operation or some event, returns to Ready once the event completes, and finally moves to Terminated after execution finishes. The operating system manages these state transitions through scheduling and resource management.

